/// A generic function that calls itself with a BIGGER type is refused, and
/// `vyrn check`, `vyrn run` and `vyrn build` refuse it with one sentence.
///
/// Monomorphization gives one function one body per distinct instantiation.
/// `f<T>` calling `f<P<T>>` asks for `f<P<Int64>>`, then `f<P<P<Int64>>>`, and
/// never repeats a type, so the worklist never empties. The `n <= 0` guard below
/// is a RUN-time test and cannot stop a COMPILE-time worklist — which is why
/// this used to pass `vyrn check` and then hang `vyrn build` forever, printing
/// nothing.
///
/// Two bounds catch it. A type may nest 64 levels, and it may have 65,536 parts
/// once its records are written out. `P` is a record of two fields, so its parts
/// double every level and the SIZE bound is the one that fires here; a spine
/// like `Array<Array<..>>` never grows wide, so the depth bound fires for that.
/// Either way the refusal names the type, which is the chain of calls written
/// down: one `P` per instantiation.
type P<T> = { a: T, b: T }

fn mk<T>(x: T) -> P<T> {
    return P { a: x, b: x }
}

fn f<T>(x: T, n: Int64) -> Int64 {
    if n <= 0 {
        return 0
    }
    return f(mk(x), n - 1)
}

fn main() -> Int64 {
    print("\{f(1, 3)}")
    return 0
}
